WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.001 --> 00:00:03.503
We often talk about the user experience,

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:07.073
but frequently
we don't talk about the people that build

00:00:07.073 --> 00:00:10.143
the user experience:
The developer experience.

00:00:11.111 --> 00:00:13.413
Developers have a lot of frustrations,

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:16.383
a lot of things that aren't writing code
that they are dealing with.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:22.088
And Spotify saw this themselves
and they decided to do something about it.

00:00:22.689 --> 00:00:28.215
So they created an open source project
donated to the CNCF called Backstage.

00:00:28.215 --> 00:00:33.133
Backstage attempts to address all of these,
but doing it slightly differently

00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:36.102
by always making sure
that the developer is in focus.

00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:40.407
So let's look at some of these pains
and how Backstage can help solve them.

00:00:41.541 --> 00:00:44.844
First off is

00:00:44.844 --> 00:00:46.446
“bookmark of death”.

00:00:46.513 --> 00:00:48.114
What do I mean by that?

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:51.418
Well, as a developer,
I've felt this myself in the past,

00:00:51.418 --> 00:00:53.553
and I feel like a lot of you
watching this have--

00:00:54.087 --> 00:00:57.023
you have a neverending list of bookmarks
that if you lost, you

00:00:57.023 --> 00:01:00.093
probably wouldn't know where to go
or how to find anything.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.462
And why do you have these?

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:05.365
Because there's no central place to find

00:01:05.365 --> 00:01:07.500
all of your websites, your applications.

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:12.505
And Backstage solves
this with the catalog.

00:01:13.139 --> 00:01:16.676
But the way it does it,
a little differently--instead of another

00:01:16.676 --> 00:01:21.314
thing to maintain, it simply leverages
the repositories you already have,

00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:25.051
let it be repository,
storing your Kubernetes projects,

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:29.856
or automation playbooks,
or virtual machine applications.

00:01:29.856 --> 00:01:32.125
You've already taken the time
to organize them and structure them,

00:01:32.826 --> 00:01:35.628
so Backstage
leverages them to create this catalog.

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:38.465
So everything is now in one place.

00:01:38.465 --> 00:01:40.800
To top that off with plug-ins, now

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.104
you can bring the technologies
that those use into the catalog.

00:01:44.604 --> 00:01:49.142
So now when you look at an item
such as your Kubernetes service,

00:01:49.142 --> 00:01:52.979
we can see the ArgosCD run or Tekton run.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:57.750
We can also see any Jira tickets,
or any other project management tickets

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.018
that you're using.

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:02.722
We might also be able to see, say,
pull requests

00:02:02.722 --> 00:02:05.792
that are waiting to be reviewed
and really anything else.

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:12.031
Now, the next thing that gets frustrating
is “ticket purgatory”.

00:02:13.266 --> 00:02:15.101
You've probably been there.

00:02:15.101 --> 00:02:16.603
You need a new repository.

00:02:16.603 --> 00:02:20.106
You need a new service or namespace
in your cluster, a new VM spun up...

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.008
something.

00:02:22.041 --> 00:02:26.179
And you wind up searching forever, trying
just to figure out who do I even ask?

00:02:26.212 --> 00:02:27.814
Then when you do, you file the ticket

00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:31.251
and hope that one day gets answered.
And you're left in purgatory.

00:02:32.018 --> 00:02:35.622
Well, Backstage solves this with
what's called "software templates".

00:02:36.356 --> 00:02:38.858
And software templates
enable your organization

00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:41.528
to create best practices
and automate them.

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:45.265
So instead of you filing a ticket
and then talking to somebody else

00:02:45.265 --> 00:02:49.169
and hoping for the best, you simply
fill out a form and software templates

00:02:49.502 --> 00:02:54.240
kicks off any of the automations necessary
to build these things.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.977
let it be your Kubernetes
namespace, new automation playbook,

00:02:58.144 --> 00:03:01.080
an entire Git repository,
or a virtual machine.

00:03:01.781 --> 00:03:05.985
And then it also makes
sure to add that into the catalog.

00:03:06.753 --> 00:03:11.791
So now that new resource that you've spun
up is expanding in the catalog for you.

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:15.295
So we've got a catalog
with all of our stuff.

00:03:15.695 --> 00:03:17.397
We can make new things.

00:03:17.397 --> 00:03:18.631
Now we have to explain what they are.

00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:20.500
And this is

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.702
where the “documentation
graveyard” comes in.

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:27.106
Write a document and you put it in a file,
put it in a folder.

00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:30.009
Maybe you put it in the master document
management system your company has,

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:31.778
and it's gone forever.

00:03:31.778 --> 00:03:33.980
You can't find it.
No one else can find it.

00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:37.450
Even if they wanted to,
they probably will never look at it again.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.752
It's not updated and it confuses everyone.

00:03:39.986 --> 00:03:43.022
You hire someone new and they don't
even know where to get started.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.125
So Backstage addresses this

00:03:46.125 --> 00:03:48.394
by simplifying the documentation problem.

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:52.565
By one, eliminating proprietary, strange

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:56.102
WYSIWYG documents
and just connecting them to the catalog

00:03:58.071 --> 00:04:01.441
and making the markdown files
that live in your repository.

00:04:01.874 --> 00:04:06.079
So now whenever you want to update
your documentation for, say,

00:04:06.079 --> 00:04:10.283
my service running Kubernetes, well,
I just do a pull request.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:12.952
And that means that
when I'm updating code,

00:04:13.052 --> 00:04:15.455
I can probably update the document
along the way.

00:04:15.455 --> 00:04:18.291
Backstage takes care of rendering
all of it together in one place.

00:04:18.825 --> 00:04:20.893
I just put it in the repository
like I do everything else.

00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:23.129
Now we've

00:04:23.129 --> 00:04:25.798
got a catalog, we've got the documents,
we got the templates.

00:04:26.165 --> 00:04:27.900
That's a lot of stuff.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.036
So how do we make this any better?

00:04:30.036 --> 00:04:34.540
Well, we tie it all together by preventing
you from being “lost at sea”.

00:04:34.807 --> 00:04:36.943
By that, I mean finding your way

00:04:38.011 --> 00:04:39.612
with Backstage's search.

00:04:39.612 --> 00:04:43.750
It indexes the catalog,
the documentations, software

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:45.285
templates and plug-ins.

00:04:45.285 --> 00:04:48.187
And from there,
you're able to search to find anything.

00:04:48.655 --> 00:04:52.692
So maybe you want to know if we have
a Kafka service that runs already,

00:04:52.992 --> 00:04:56.696
or if you want to know who owns
that website and everything in between.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:01.634
Because the search is building
off the material that you as the developer

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:05.038
are helping add through the repository
you're already managing,

00:05:05.605 --> 00:05:07.707
that means you can find it
and it's always up-to-date.

00:05:08.574 --> 00:05:11.644
So Backstage is able to address
that problem with the “bookmarks

00:05:11.644 --> 00:05:15.315
and death” by giving you a catalog to pull
all of your stuff from your repository

00:05:15.448 --> 00:05:16.382
and make it easy to find.

00:05:17.317 --> 00:05:18.217
It gives you a way to

00:05:18.217 --> 00:05:20.553
create those new resources
through software templates.

00:05:21.321 --> 00:05:22.889
It gives you a way
to keep up with documents

00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:24.490
so they don't go to the graveyard.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:26.592
And it prevents everyone from being lost

00:05:26.592 --> 00:05:29.262
by indexing all of it
and making it easy to find.

00:05:30.063 --> 00:05:33.900
If you want to learn more about Backstage
and how it can benefit your organization

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:37.970
or how to contribute to itself,
go to backstage.io.

00:05:38.671 --> 00:05:43.109
From there you'll find tutorials
and examples of how to deploy Backstage

00:05:43.409 --> 00:05:45.678
and how to make best practices
for your organization.

00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:50.149
And if you want a fully supported,
scaled enterprise-capable

00:05:50.416 --> 00:05:55.588
and secured and compliant version,
Red Hat Developer Hub might be for you.

00:05:56.255 --> 00:05:58.591
This is Red Hat's enterprise-hardened

00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:02.462
instance of Backstage
with our own hardened plug-ins.

00:06:03.229 --> 00:06:08.334
To learn more about that,
go to developers.redhat.com/rhdh.

00:06:09.168 --> 00:06:10.503
Thanks for watching.

00:06:10.503 --> 00:06:13.873
